  "contracts": {
    "def-emg-lorentzian-forms": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "**(a) Lorentzian metric.** The field $g$ is a **Lorentzian metric of signature $(-,+,+,+)$** on $M$ when it is symmetric and nondegenerate, and at every $p\\in M$ the pair $(T_pM,g_p)$ is a Lorentz vector space of signature $(-,+,+,+)$ in the sense of [[def-lorentz-form-and-inertia]]: that is, $g_p$ has inertia $(3,1,0)$, so that some — equivalently every — basis of $T_pM$ in which the matrix of $g_p$ is diagonal has exactly one negative and three positive diagonal entries.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-oriented-smooth-manifold-and-oriented-chart",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "An orientation of an $n$-manifold is a smooth choice of a ray in $\\det T_pM$ for every $p$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-gr-observer-and-rest-space",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "**(a) Observers.** An **observer at $p$** is a tangent vector $n\\in T_pM$ such that $g_p(n,n)=-1$ and $n$ is future-directed in the sense of [[def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects]], that is, $g_p(n,N_p)<0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-gr-observer-projection-decomposition",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "$\\dim n^{\\perp}=3$ and the restriction of $g_p$ to $n^{\\perp}$ is positive definite.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-gr-observer-congruence-and-adapted-charts",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "**(a) Congruences.** An **observer congruence** on $U$ is a smooth vector field $n$ on $U$ ([[def-smooth-vector-field-as-a-tangent-bundle-section]]) such that $n_q$ is an observer at $q$ for every $q\\in U$ ([[def-gr-observer-and-rest-space]]); explicitly, $$g_q(n_q,n_q)=-1\\quad\\text{and}\\quad g_q(n_q,N_q)<0\\qquad\\text{for every }q\\in U .$$ Since $g_q(n_q,n_q)=-1$, the field $n$ is nowhere vanishing.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-gr-local-frame-and-tetrad-fields",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "**(c) Tetrad fields.** A **tetrad field** (or **$g$-orthonormal frame field**) on $U$ is a local frame field $(E_0,E_1,E_2,E_3)$ on $U$ such that $$g_q(E_A(q),E_B(q))=\\eta_{AB}\\qquad\\text{for all }A,B\\text{ and all }q\\in U .$$ A tetrad field is **future-directed** when in addition $E_0(q)$ is future-directed for every $q\\in U$ ([[def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects]]); then each $E_0(q)$ is an observer at $q$ ([[def-gr-observer-and-rest-space]]), so $E_0$ is an observer congruence on $U$ ([[def-gr-observer-congruence-and-adapted-charts]]), while $E_1,E_2,E_3$ are pointwise an orthonormal basis of the rest space of that observer.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-gr-metric-selected-connection",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "**(c) Metric-selected connections.** A **metric-selected connection** for $(M,g)$ is an affine connection on $M$ that is both torsion-free and $g$-compatible.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-gr-curvature-tensoriality-and-coordinate-formula",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "**(b) Coordinate formula.** In every smooth chart $(U,x)$ with Christoffel symbols $\\Gamma^a{}_{bc}$ of $\\nabla$ ([[def-christoffel-symbols-of-an-affine-connection]]), $$R^a{}_{bcd}=\\partial_c\\Gamma^a{}_{db}-\\partial_d\\Gamma^a{}_{cb} +\\sum_e\\bigl(\\Gamma^a{}_{ce}\\Gamma^e{}_{db}-\\Gamma^a{}_{de}\\Gamma^e{}_{cb}\\bigr)$$ on $U$, for all $a,b,c,d\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-real-power-continuity-and-derivatives",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For $a>0$, the function $x\\mapsto a^x$ is continuous on $\\mathbb R$ and $$(a^x)'=a^x\\log a.$$ For $\\alpha\\in\\mathbb R$, the function $x\\mapsto x^\\alpha$ is continuous and differentiable on $(0,\\infty)$, with $$(x^\\alpha)'=\\alpha x^{\\alpha-1}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following statement.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "D1",
          "claim": "The normalization $n:=N/\\sqrt{-g(N,N)}$ is smooth because $-g(N,N)$ is a smooth positive function and [F5] makes its positive square root smooth. Bilinearity gives $g(n,n)=-1$ and $g(n,N)=-\\sqrt{-g(N,N)}<0$, so [F2] makes $n$ an observer at every point and hence a smooth observer congruence. This supplies an observer field without identifying it with a chart or tetrad.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F5",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "D2",
          "claim": "Fix $p\\in U$ and a connected chart around $p$. The four coordinate vectors form a basis at $p$. By [F2], the linear map $h_{n_p}$ has image the three-dimensional space $n_p^\\perp$, so three projected coordinate vectors form a basis of $n_p^\\perp$. Extend the chosen coordinate fields over the chart and project them by $h_n$; after shrinking the chart, their projections remain independent by continuity. They lie in the positive-definite rest spaces by [F2]. Apply the three finite Gram-Schmidt operations in these rest spaces; every denominator is the positive square root of a positive smooth squared norm, so [F5] makes the resulting fields smooth. Together with $E_0=n$, the three fields form a local tetrad. If needed, reverse one spatial member so the tetrad has the supplied orientation from [F1]; the sign remains fixed on the connected chart. This is a local tetrad field as in [F3], not a coordinate frame or an assertion of an adapted coordinate chart. Thus an $n$-adapted tetrad exists locally, with no global tetrad or hypersurface-orthogonality conclusion.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F5",
            "A1",
            "construct",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "D3",
          "claim": "In a length-coordinate chart, each first coordinate derivative of $g_{ab}$ has dimension $\\mathrm m^{-1}$, so the Christoffel formula in [F4] has dimension $\\mathrm m^{-1}$. Each term in the curvature formula in [F4] has dimension $\\mathrm m^{-2}$; contraction with dimensionless $g^{ab}$ preserves that dimension for Ricci and scalar curvature. This is coordinate-unit bookkeeping, not a physical field equation.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F4",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
