    "lem-emg-lorentz-hodge-calculus": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-emg-lorentzian-forms",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For every observer congruence $n$, each point has a neighborhood carrying an $n$-adapted tetrad; this is a local assertion only.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "2.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-gr-local-frame-and-tetrad-fields",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "**(c) Tetrad fields.** A **tetrad field** (or **$g$-orthonormal frame field**) on $U$ is a local frame field $(E_0,E_1,E_2,E_3)$ on $U$ such that $$g_q(E_A(q),E_B(q))=\\eta_{AB}\\qquad\\text{for all }A,B\\text{ and all }q\\in U .$$ A tetrad field is **future-directed** when in addition $E_0(q)$ is future-directed for every $q\\in U$ ([[def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects]]); then each $E_0(q)$ is an observer at $q$ ([[def-gr-observer-and-rest-space]]), so $E_0$ is an observer congruence on $U$ ([[def-gr-observer-congruence-and-adapted-charts]]), while $E_1,E_2,E_3$ are pointwise an orthonormal basis of the rest space of that observer.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "2.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-gr-metric-volume-form-and-current-flux",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "That these local forms agree on chart overlaps and therefore define one smooth nowhere-vanishing $4$-form $\\mathrm{vol}_g$ on $M$ — the **metric volume form** — is proved in [[lem-gr-metric-volume-form-patching-and-face-normal]] clause (a), together with the reading statement of clause (b) there: in every oriented chart $\\mathrm{vol}_g=w\\,dx^0\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge dx^3$, and on every positively oriented $g_p$-orthonormal basis $(e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3)$ of $T_pM$ one has $\\mathrm{vol}_{g,p}(e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3)=1$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "2.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-gr-metric-volume-form-patching-and-face-normal",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Then $$\\det(g'_{ab})=\\bigl(\\det D\\varphi\\bigr)^{2}\\det\\bigl(g_{ab}\\circ\\varphi\\bigr),\\qquad w'=\\bigl(\\det D\\varphi\\bigr)\\,(w\\circ\\varphi),$$ and the local forms agree on the overlap, $$\\varphi^{*}\\bigl(w\\,dx^0\\wedge dx^1\\wedge dx^2\\wedge dx^3\\bigr)=w'\\,dy^0\\wedge dy^1\\wedge dy^2\\wedge dy^3 .$$ Consequently there is a unique smooth nowhere-vanishing $4$-form $\\mathrm{vol}_g$ on $M$ — the **metric volume form** — whose restriction to the domain of every oriented chart $(U,x)$ is $w\\,dx^0\\wedge dx^1\\wedge dx^2\\wedge dx^3$; it is a smooth section with nowhere-zero values and positive on the given orientation.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "2.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-smooth-differential-k-form",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A **smooth differential $k$-form** on $M$ is a smooth section of $\\bigwedge^kT^*M\\to M$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "prop-local-coordinate-expression-for-a-differential-form",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "On a chart $(U,x^1,\\ldots,x^n)$, every smooth differential $k$-form $\\omega$ has a unique expression $$ \\omega=\\sum_{1\\le i_1<\\cdots<i_k\\le n}\\omega_{i_1\\cdots i_k}\\,dx^{i_1}\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge dx^{i_k} $$ with smooth coefficient functions $\\omega_{i_1\\cdots i_k}$ on $U$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-wedge-product-of-differential-forms",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Their **wedge product** is the differential form defined pointwise by $$ (\\alpha\\wedge\\beta)_p:=\\alpha_p\\wedge\\beta_p\\in\\operatorname{Alt}^{k+\\ell}(T_pM).",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "prop-differential-forms-form-a-graded-commutative-algebra",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The graded vector space $$ \\Omega^*(M):=\\bigoplus_{k\\ge 0}\\Omega^k(M) $$ with the wedge product is an associative graded-commutative algebra.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-exterior-derivative-by-the-invariant-vector-field-formula",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $\\omega\\in\\Omega^k(M)$, define the candidate $d\\omega$ on smooth vector fields by $$d\\omega(X_0,\\ldots,X_k)=\\sum_i(-1)^iX_i\\omega(X_0,\\widehat X_i,\\ldots,X_k)+\\sum_{i<j}(-1)^{i+j}\\omega([X_i,X_j],X_0,\\widehat X_i,\\widehat X_j,\\ldots,X_k).$$ The next lemma proves that this candidate is a form.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-the-invariant-exterior-derivative-formula-is-c-infinity-multilinear",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The invariant formula defining $d\\omega$ is alternating and $C^\\infty(M)$-multilinear in $X_0,\\ldots,X_k$; hence it defines a smooth $(k+1)$-form.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-local-coordinate-formula-for-the-exterior-derivative",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Summing over increasing $k$-tuples $I$, and writing $dx^I=dx^{i_1}\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge dx^{i_k}$, if $\\omega=\\sum_I\\omega_I\\,dx^I$, then $$d\\omega=\\sum_I d\\omega_I\\wedge dx^I.$$",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-clairaut-schwarz-mixed-partials",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "If $f$ is $C^2$ on an open subset of $\\mathbb R^m$, then $\\partial_i\\partial_j f=\\partial_j\\partial_i f$ for every pair of coordinate indices.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-the-exterior-derivative-is-a-graded-derivation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For homogeneous smooth forms $\\alpha\\in\\Omega^p(M)$ and $\\beta\\in\\Omega^q(M)$, $$d(\\alpha\\wedge\\beta)=d\\alpha\\wedge\\beta+(-1)^{\\deg\\alpha}\\alpha\\wedge d\\beta.$$",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-gr-inverse-metric-and-musical-isomorphisms",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "**Inverse metric.** The **inverse metric** at $p$ is the symmetric bilinear form $$g_p^{-1}:T_p^*M\\times T_p^*M\\longrightarrow\\mathbb R,\\qquad g_p^{-1}(\\alpha,\\beta):=g_p(\\sharp_p\\alpha,\\sharp_p\\beta).$$ It is symmetric because $g_p$ is, and it is nondegenerate: if $g_p^{-1}(\\alpha,\\beta)=0$ for all $\\beta$, then $g_p(\\sharp_p\\alpha,\\cdot)=0$, so $\\sharp_p\\alpha=0$ and hence $\\alpha=0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following statement.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "D1",
          "claim": "The determinant is alternating multilinear in each list, so it defines a bilinear pairing on exterior powers. In a positively oriented orthonormal coframe, it makes the basis wedges $e^I$ orthogonal with squared norm $\\epsilon_I$; in the full component contraction this same diagonal value occurs $k!$ times, canceled by the factor $1/k!$. Define $\\star_g e^I=\\epsilon_I s(I,I^c)e^{I^c}$ and extend linearly. If $J\\ne I$ has the same size as $I$, then $e^J\\wedge e^{I^c}=0$ because an index repeats. If $J=I$, then $e^I\\wedge e^{I^c}=s(I,I^c)\\mathrm{vol}_g$, so $e^I\\wedge\\star_g e^I=\\epsilon_I\\mathrm{vol}_g$. Bilinearity proves the characterizing identity for all forms in the coframe. The complement wedges pair each basis vector with exactly one basis vector, so this wedge pairing is nondegenerate and the characterization determines $\\star_g$ uniquely.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F6",
            "A1",
            "algebra",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "D2",
          "claim": "The characterizing identity uses only $g$, the orientation, and $\\mathrm{vol}_g$. Any other positively oriented orthonormal coframe therefore defines the same map by uniqueness. In each smooth tetrad neighborhood the displayed matrix of $\\star_g$ is constant in the smooth coframe, so its coordinate coefficients are smooth; the locally defined maps agree on overlaps and give a global smooth bundle map on the inherited smooth background [A1].",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "A1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "D3",
          "claim": "Applying the basis formula twice gives $\\star_g^2e^I=\\epsilon_I\\epsilon_{I^c}s(I,I^c)s(I^c,I)e^I$. The product $\\epsilon_I\\epsilon_{I^c}$ equals $-1$ because exactly one of the four $\\epsilon_i$ is negative. Reversing the two blocks of sizes $k$ and $4-k$ takes $k(4-k)$ transpositions, so $s(I,I^c)s(I^c,I)=(-1)^{k(4-k)}$. Their product is $(-1)^{k(4-k)+1}$, proving the square formula for each basis element and hence for every $k$-form.",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "D4",
          "claim": "For a two-form $F=\\frac12F_{cd}e^c\\wedge e^d$, the basis formula sends each $e^c\\wedge e^d$ to its oriented complementary wedge with the product of its metric signs. In components this is $({\\star_gF})_{ab}=\\frac12(\\mathrm{vol}_g)_{abcd}F^{cd}$: the factor $1/2$ counts each antisymmetric pair once, and raising both indices includes the negative sign in the timelike slot.",
          "step": "2.3",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "F1",
            "F6",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "D5",
          "claim": "In a chart, [F4] gives $(d\\alpha)_{a_0\\cdots a_k}=(k+1)\\partial_{[a_0}\\alpha_{a_1\\cdots a_k]}$. Applying it twice expresses each coefficient of $d^2\\alpha$ as an alternating sum of second partial derivatives; terms paired by exchanging the first two derivative indices cancel because those mixed partials agree by [F5]. Thus $d^2\\alpha=0$ in every chart and hence globally. The graded product rule is exactly the established identity in [F5].",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
