    "lem-emg-conformal-hodge-multiplier": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-emg-lorentz-hodge-calculus",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "There is a unique smooth bundle map $\\star_g:\\Omega^k(M)\\to\\Omega^{4-k}(M)$ characterized by $$\\alpha\\wedge\\star_g\\beta=\\langle\\alpha,\\beta\\rangle_g\\,\\mathrm{vol}_g$$ at every point.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following statement.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "D1",
          "claim": "On the inherited smooth manifold background [A1], since $\\widetilde g=\\Omega^2g$ and $\\Omega>0$, its inverse is $\\widetilde g^{-1}=\\Omega^{-2}g^{-1}$. The exterior pairing of two $k$-forms contains exactly $k$ inverse-metric factors, so $\\langle\\alpha,\\beta\\rangle_{\\widetilde g}=\\Omega^{-2k}\\langle\\alpha,\\beta\\rangle_g$. In a positively oriented chart, $\\det(\\widetilde g_{ab})=\\Omega^8\\det(g_{ab})$; therefore the metric volume form scales as $\\mathrm{vol}_{\\widetilde g}=\\Omega^4\\mathrm{vol}_g$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "A1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "D2",
          "claim": "Applying [F1] to $\\widetilde g$ and substituting step 1.1 gives $\\alpha\\wedge\\star_{\\widetilde g}\\beta=\\Omega^{-2k}\\langle\\alpha,\\beta\\rangle_g\\,\\Omega^4\\mathrm{vol}_g=\\alpha\\wedge\\bigl(\\Omega^{4-2k}\\star_g\\beta\\bigr)$. The pointwise wedge pairing is nondegenerate, so the form paired with every $\\alpha$ is unique; hence $\\star_{\\widetilde g}\\beta=\\Omega^{4-2k}\\star_g\\beta$. For $k=0,1,2,3,4$ the multipliers are respectively $\\Omega^4,\\Omega^2,1,\\Omega^{-2},\\Omega^{-4}$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
