    "lem-emg-observer-field-decomposition": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-emg-lorentzian-forms",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For every observer congruence $n$, each point has a neighborhood carrying an $n$-adapted tetrad; this is a local assertion only.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-gr-observer-and-rest-space",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "**(a) Observers.** An **observer at $p$** is a tangent vector $n\\in T_pM$ such that $g_p(n,n)=-1$ and $n$ is future-directed in the sense of [[def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects]], that is, $g_p(n,N_p)<0$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-gr-observer-projection-decomposition",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "$\\dim n^{\\perp}=3$ and the restriction of $g_p$ to $n^{\\perp}$ is positive definite.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-emg-lorentz-hodge-calculus",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "There is a unique smooth bundle map $\\star_g:\\Omega^k(M)\\to\\Omega^{4-k}(M)$ characterized by $$\\alpha\\wedge\\star_g\\beta=\\langle\\alpha,\\beta\\rangle_g\\,\\mathrm{vol}_g$$ at every point.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-gr-inverse-metric-and-musical-isomorphisms",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "**Inverse metric.** The **inverse metric** at $p$ is the symmetric bilinear form $$g_p^{-1}:T_p^*M\\times T_p^*M\\longrightarrow\\mathbb R,\\qquad g_p^{-1}(\\alpha,\\beta):=g_p(\\sharp_p\\alpha,\\sharp_p\\beta).$$ It is symmetric because $g_p$ is, and it is nondegenerate: if $g_p^{-1}(\\alpha,\\beta)=0$ for all $\\beta$, then $g_p(\\sharp_p\\alpha,\\cdot)=0$, so $\\sharp_p\\alpha=0$ and hence $\\alpha=0$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "prop-differential-forms-form-a-graded-commutative-algebra",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The graded vector space $$ \\Omega^*(M):=\\bigoplus_{k\\ge 0}\\Omega^k(M) $$ with the wedge product is an associative graded-commutative algebra.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following statement.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "D1",
          "claim": "Antisymmetry of $F$ gives $E_an^a=cF_{ab}n^an^b=0$. The Hodge dual is also a two-form, so $B_an^a=-({\\star_gF})_{ab}n^an^b=0$. Thus both covectors restrict to the positive rest covector space.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "D2",
          "claim": "Fix a point and use [F1], on the inherited background [A1], to choose a positively oriented orthonormal coframe $(e^0,e^1,e^2,e^3)$ with $n^\\flat=-e^0$. The definition gives $F_{0i}=-E_i/c$. For $i=1,2,3$, the component formula in [F2] gives $({\\star_gF})_{10}=-F_{23}$, $({\\star_gF})_{20}=-F_{31}$, and $({\\star_gF})_{30}=-F_{12}$; therefore $B_1=F_{23}$, $B_2=F_{31}$, and $B_3=F_{12}$, equivalently $F_{ij}=\\epsilon_{ijk}B_k$. The basis formula in [F2] gives $\\star_g(-e^0\\wedge B)=B_1e^2\\wedge e^3+B_2e^3\\wedge e^1+B_3e^1\\wedge e^2$. Together with $n^\\flat\\wedge E/c=-\\sum_i(E_i/c)e^0\\wedge e^i$, this matches all six independent components of $F$ and proves $F=n^\\flat\\wedge E/c+\\star_g(n^\\flat\\wedge B)$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "A1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "D3",
          "claim": "In this coframe, $F^{0i}=-F_{0i}=E_i/c$ and $F^{ij}=F_{ij}$. Antisymmetry counts each independent pair twice, so $F_{ab}F^{ab}=2\\sum_{i<j}F_{ij}^2+2\\sum_iF_{0i}F^{0i}=2\\sum_iB_i^2-\\frac{2}{c^2}\\sum_iE_i^2$. The spatial sums are precisely the squared norms on the positive rest space, proving the first invariant.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 2.1",
            "F3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "D4",
          "claim": "Write $F=-\\sum_i(E_i/c)e^0\\wedge e^i+B_1e^{23}+B_2e^{31}+B_3e^{12}$. The pure electric and pure magnetic terms wedge to zero. By [F4], each matching electric/magnetic pair occurs twice and contributes $-2E_iB_i\\,\\mathrm{vol}_g/c$. Therefore $F\\wedge F=-(2/c)\\langle E,B\\rangle_n\\mathrm{vol}_g$. Both invariant identities are tensorial, so the local calculation holds at every point of $U$.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 2.1",
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
